Sigh!  Posting such cathegorical recipies leads only to "cargo cult
computer usage" and/or "cargo cult programming".  (If you do not
know what "cargo cult" is seek in anthropology materials).

Older versions of fdisk had off-by-one bug and when used to write
BSD-type labels they were proposing an upper limit for the last
partition one higher then a reported few lines earlier a total number
of sectors on a disk.  If you would use the lower number instead
of a proposed "default" you would be fine; otherwise the last partition
would "wrap around" with nasty consequences if you would ever write
anything in this last sector.  Current versions of fdisk do not have
this bug therefore do not use obsolete variants of fdisk (or pay
a close attention to what is going on).

When booting via SRM you also need a space for a boot block IF
you are ever going to boot from that disk; otherwise you do not care.
Also if you have "DU-compatible" partitioning on that disk then you
have an "unused" partition 'c' which covers all sectors of disk.
As this is not a "data partition" the fact that some of its sectors
can be overlaid by a boot block is not doing any harm.  You indeed
do not want to do that with your data or swap or you will have
a "food fight" for a disk space on hands.

  Michal



