Most Alpha's (pre-EV6) work similarly in that floating point exceptions
are asynchronous. You need to compile with "/fpe:3"
or "/syncronous_exceptions" (Visual FORTRAN compiler options). This
will tell the compiler to put a TRAPB instruction after every floating
point instruction to flush the pipelines before the next instruction is
executed. This kills performance and you shouldn't do it in
a "production" program unless you have a very good reason for doing so
and understand why you are doing it.

Jeff D


In article <q85mq8.6mh.ln@robpc1.telus.net>,
  Rob Komar <rkomar@telus.net> wrote:
> Don Baker <don.baker@aquarien.com> wrote:
> >   Yes, Compaq sells Fortran compilers, but do they sell a good
debugger
> > to go with them?  The last I heard, Compaq Visual Fortran version 6
in
> > Windows still cannot find the source code for a divide-by-zero, or
log
> > or exponent of a negative number runtime error.  It's extremely
> > aggravating when one is developing numerical method programs for
> > nonlinear PDEs.  When it was DEC Visual Fortran, DEC used to blame
> > Windows for that.  Does anyone know if they also have the same
problem
> > in Linux?
>
> > Don Baker
>
> We do.  The problem is that some FPU (floating-point unit) operations
take
> a long time.  To save some time, other kinds of instructions that
don't
> depend on the outcome of the FP operation are often processed by the
CPU
> while the FPU is working.  If a FPU error occurs, the program counter
> can have moved on by many instructions by the time the signal from the
> hardware interrupt from the FPU is recognized by the program.  To get
> around this on X86 machines, an FWAIT instruction needs to be inserted
> after each floating-point operation to make the CPU wait for the FPU
to
> finish before going on to the next instruction.  As far as I know, the
> gcc compilers don't do this.  Bill Metzenthen wrote some external code
> to do this (see http://www.suburbia.net/~billm), but it works on the
> assembler output from gcc, so it requires a few extra steps to produce
> an executable (ie. it's a bit of a pain).
>
> I've never used Compaq Visual Fortran, but it's possible that it can
> do this.  I'd look in the documentation for a description of a switch
> that adds FWAIT instructions to the code (assuming your using an X86
> machine and not an Alpha).  I'd also compile the code with no
optimization
> at all to keep instruction re-ordering to a minimum.
>
> Cheers,
> Rob Komar
>


