The information you found is not exactly true.
Linux itself does, in fact, support headless boot, if you build it into the
kernel.
Problem is, I've never seen any distribution that has this built into the
installation.
Needing to do this, here's what I did:

(You can read all this stuff below, but believe me, it's far easier to
locate a graphics card for installation purposes, and only use the headless
mode later on, when the system is installed)
The easiest thing to do is:  Find a graphics card to use for installation,
and afterward, put the machine into headless mode.

Using another linux box, build a kernel.  Pay particular attention to
    Character Devices,
    virtual term
    console on virtual term
    standard/generic (dumb) serial support
    support for console on serial port

Next, I downloaded the rescue and root floppies from

ftp://ftp.debian.org/pub/Linux/distributions/debian/dists/stable/main/disks-alpha/current

Also download the base and drivers.  Available as large tarballs or disk
images.

On the rescue disk, replace the old kernel with the recently built one.

The next problem was:
When I boot with this command
    b dva -fi whatever -fl "root=/dev/fd0 console=ttyS0,9600 load_ramdisk=1"

there's a problem
The ramdisk prompt won't take keyboard input from a serial line.  Instead
use:
    b dva -fi whatever -fl "root=/dev/fd0 console=ttyS0,9600 load_ramdisk=1
prompt_ramdisk=0"
You have to watch the led, and swap disks when it stops, without waiting for
the prompt.

A couple more pitfalls to expect:
I think the debian install doesn't put a getty on serial, so you might have
to modify /etc/inittab and/or install a copy of getty.
The kernel may not recognize the name 'ttyS0' so you might have to look up
the major and minor numbers.
Things might still fail.  I did it about a couple months ago, and I don't
remember exactly how things turned out.  I might have given in and used the
graphics card.

