I think that your problem has nothing to do with raid.  If hurried, just read
the last sentence and skip all the rest.

A few things, just to make sure I understand:
- You don't intend to stripe the root or boot partition.
- You will have two separate copies of the root partition (/dev/sda1 /dev/sdb1),
and you will only mount one of them.  Presumably, this is for redundancy and
security of your data.
- You will have two separate copies of the boot partition (/dev/sda2 /dev/sdb2),
and you will only mount one of them.  Presumably, this is for redundancy and
security of your data.
- I'm surprised that DKC0 corresponds with sda1.

A few things, just to make sure you understand:
- Some people say that you should never use the first or last cylinder of a
disk.  This is a very small loss, and even if it's false, it doesn't hurt to do
it.
- SRM cannot mount anything.
- (Usually) SRM loads aboot.  aboot can read ext2 or simply move to the next
sector.  It needs to find a kernel.  Your kernel must have RAID support, and
must have auto RAID support.  See the Software RAID HOWTO.  There are at least 3
howto's about raid, boot raid, and boot root raid.
- Assuming you want to use aboot, you need to use swriteboot to make a disk
bootable.  This will clobber some of the first cylinder.  Until you do this, SRM
will always say "not a valid boot block"  Performing this task has nothing to do
with RAID.

