Good luck with your floppy.  I can't help with that.

If it doesn't work out, and you need to boot from another disk, or from cdrom,
this might help:

If you're booting from another disk, I assume you've got another computer working
that you can use to set up that disk.  Why not simply install to the disk while
it's in the other computer, and just move the disk over to this one?  A couple of
things to remember are:  remember to edit your fstab, incase the drive location
is changing.  ie from sda1 to sdb1 or something.  You'll need to know the boot
command, too.

If you're booting from CD, I assume you've either got a CD burner, or an official
purchased release of some distro.  If burning the CD yourself, trust me, it's
worth while to download the entire ISO image, rather than learning to choose
certain files, create an ISO image, and make it bootable.  Just download the ISO
image from somewhere, and burn that.  Once you have a bootable CD, booting from
it should be relatively trivial.  Ask if you have trouble.

One more thing -- You'll get more specific information if you specify to the
group, which distro you want to install.  If you can't decide, say "I want to
know which distro would be good, where to get the iso image, and how to boot from
that CD."

