Type 'setterm vt100'.  

It looks like that your setup is not very careful about how it
sets its terminals.  You can do that in your startup files (say,
include from /etc/profile).   Here is one example how you are doing
such things:

if [ "$TERM" = "" -o "$TERM" = "unknown" -o \
 "`echo $TERM | cut -b1-3`" = "con" ]; then
 TERM=linux
fi
mytty=`tty`
if [ "$LOG_TERM" != "$TERM" ] ; then
   if [ -n "$mytty" ] ; then
       if expr "$mytty" : '/dev/ttyS' >/dev/null ; then
            eval `tset -s -Q -m :?vt100`
            unset LINES
            unset TERMCAP
       fi
   fi
   [ "$TERM" = vt100-am ] && TERM=vt100
   export LOG_TERM=$TERM
fi
[ "$TERM" = vt100-am ] && TERM=vt100
[ $mytty ] && unset mytty

Adjust to your needs and a taste.

   Michal




