Um, you _should_ be typing:

>>> boot dka0 -fl 0
                * not i *

'-fi' is used to specify a filename from which to boot other than the
default.
e.g.,  '-fi "vmunix.mine"' (for Tru64)

The device name(s) dka0 and dka100 are correct.  'dk' means 'disk'.
'a' is the first SCSI bus adapter (usually the internal bus).  '0' is
'first
device on the bus (#0)'.  '100' is 'second device on the bus (#1)'.  The
last
two digits will be nonzero when talking 'through' storage array
adapters; in
Tru64, booting from internal storage, they are always '00'.

The naming conventions always struck me as non-intuitive.  But then, I
was
never a VMS user after 1983.   8-)    With no Alpha Linux system at
hand, I
can't suggest what the correct filename should be.  Sorry.

Corrections gladly accepted,
=Aaron

