First thing, because you used "set boot_file" and "set bootdef_dev" you
shouldn't use "boot dva0"  You should just say "boot" or "b"

When you say "boot dva0" the system will read the kernel from the floppy
disk, and use the sda1 as the root directory.  Although this isn't the way
you're supposed to do it, it should work anyway.  When you say "boot" by
itself, it will load aboot and the kernel from sda1, and then use sda1 as
the root directory.  This should also work.

So despite everything I said above, you've still got a problem:
You need to find out where init resides.  Meaning which partition.
Here's one way to find out:
    aboot knows enough to be able to read an ext2 partition, and do dir
commands.
    try:  boot -fl i
    This starts aboot in interactive mode, which will let you find the right
partition.  Any partition that looks like the root partition is the right
one.
    Then, change "set boot_osflags root=/dev/sda_" to the proper one, and
you should be ok.

There's some chance that I'm wrong about why your system doesn't work.  Let
me know how things turn out.

