[snip]

You'll definitely need the TGA server.  And it may help if the kernel is
compiled with TGA frame buffer support - it may be by default on the
Alpha distro, I don't know.

Then check out the following:
ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/X11/XF86_TGA-3.1.2-README  It
applies mostly to an older version of the TGA server, but it may still
be of help even if it doesn't enable all the hardware acceleration
available with the newer server. The XF86Config sample it provides was
very helpful to me in the past. An alternative is to try anXious,
Debian's newer X configuration utility. It seemed to work OK with the
Elsa Gloria I've got in my AS 500.

Make sure you don't have a TGA2 card.  Despite the similarity in the
name it's nothing like the TGA at all, and there are no X servers that
support it.

