"b.stolk" wrote:
> I tried very hard to make a NVIDIA TNT card work on my alpha.
> I failed.
> 
> Main reason: The flash rom on gfx cards contain code.
> This is x86 code.

Most (all?) video cards for PCs have x86 boot ROM code.

> However, the emulator for your alpha (which if I recall
> correctly) is included in MILO, only does 8086 emulation.
> It cannot handle the 16 and 32 bit opcodes.

The emulator is in the console firmware, either SRM or AlphaBIOS.  (MILO
does have an x86 emulator as well, which I guess is necessary to put
devices in a known state after AlphaBIOS does its damage for NT. 
Actually MILO had two emulators, the "free" x86 emulator with source
code, and an older object-only DEC emulator found in older MILOs.)

> Now, in order to make a gfx card with a ROM containing
> these opcodes (as nVidia's cards do) work on your alpha
> you could use a third party emulator, which I think
> was called em86.

No.  You need device support in your console firmware.  Em86 won't help,
fixing MILO won't help.  If Compaq/API fix SRM to support this card, you
don't need MILO to boot. They won't fix AlphaBIOS since Alpha/NT is
dead.

That said, if you can figure out a way to initialize the nVidia card
from the Linux kernel or even after boot, it might work since SRM
doesn't need a graphics console anyway.  But I suspect it won't be
easy.  You need to emulate real mode, and I don't think em86 was
intended for that.  Bochs <uri:http://www.bochs.com/> might help
somehow.

Jeff


