 Has LIDS been ported to Alpha?  I thought some of it was x86 specific?
 Are Solar Designer's Openwall patches portable to Alpha?


 You didn't mention aide, tripwire, viperdb, et al (file integrity
 check systems). 

>  - remote administration: secure shell (www.openssh.com or www.ssh.com)
>      using sll (www.openssl.org);
>  - web-certificates: https for apache, mod_ssl (www.modssl.org).
>  - tunneling of misc. protocols with ssl (e.g. s-imap): stunnel or sslwrap.


>    -- josef.

 Of course a more important element for improving the security
 of your systems is not what you install, but what you choose 
 NOT to install, and how you configure EVERYTHING that you do
 install.  The most important step is to define your requirements.

 Security is the enforcement of policy.  Without a clearly understood
 and defined policy any attempts to implement security measures are
 chasing your own tail (and vainly trying to implement some sort of
 "Best Practice" in some implicit/generic policy).

 A policies are requirements that detail who should have what form of 
 access to which resources.  The are sets of constraints about 
 the *availability*, *confidentiality* and *integrity*  of various 
 resources, and they define the authority hierarchies (ownership) of
 those resources. Resources include files, program execution, and many 
 intagibles such as communications channels, bandwidth, CPU time, storage 
 space, etc.


 So, the best "security system" you can "get and install" for your
 system is someone who understands systems security.  I like 
 the Counterpane System's corporate motto (Bruce Schneier):

	Security is a process; not a product.






